{
 "lead": "How a petrol engine burns depends first on its fuel. Volatility decides whether the fuel evaporates in time, octane number decides how well it resists knock, and calorific value and the chemically correct air–fuel ratio decide how much energy each cylinder full of charge carries. Even with the same fuel and settings, no two cycles burn alike: peak pressure scatters from cycle to cycle. This chapter covers both ideas, which some syllabi list as \"basic fuel properties of gasoline\" and \"cycle-to-cycle variations\".",
 "prereq": "You need: the stages of SI combustion and the mass fraction burned (<a href=\"#stages-of-combustion-and-flame-propagation-in-si-engines\">stages of combustion</a>), and flame speed (<a href=\"#flame-velocity-flame-front-and-rate-of-pressure-rise\">flame velocity</a>).",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Gasoline and its properties",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Gasoline (petrol) is a blend of a few hundred hydrocarbons, mostly with 4 to 12 carbon atoms: paraffins, iso-paraffins, olefins, naphthenes and aromatics. Indian pump petrol now also carries ethanol, up to 20 % by volume (E20). The properties an engine cares about are these."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Property",
      "Typical value",
      "Why it matters"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Density at 15 °C",
       "720 to 775 kg/m³",
       "fuel is metered by volume; energy per litre"
      ],
      [
       "Lower calorific value",
       "about 44 MJ/kg",
       "energy released per kg burnt"
      ],
      [
       "Stoichiometric air–fuel ratio",
       "about 14.7 : 1 by mass",
       "sets the mixture for complete burning and for the three-way catalyst"
      ],
      [
       "Octane number",
       "regular about 91 RON, premium 95 or more",
       "resistance to knock"
      ],
      [
       "Latent heat of vaporisation",
       "about 350 kJ/kg",
       "cools the charge as it evaporates"
      ],
      [
       "Sulphur",
       "10 ppm or less (BS-VI)",
       "protects the catalyst; less SO<sub>2</sub>"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Volatility</b> is the readiness to evaporate. It is described by the <b>distillation curve</b> (the temperature at which 10 %, 50 % and 90 % of a sample has boiled off) and by the <b>Reid vapour pressure</b>, measured at 37.8 °C. The front end (10 % point) decides cold starting, and if it is too volatile it causes vapour lock in hot fuel lines. The middle (50 % point) decides warm-up and smooth acceleration. The tail (90 % point) decides whether heavy ends wash past the rings into the oil, leave deposits and waste fuel."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Octane number</b> is the percentage by volume of iso-octane (rated 100) in a blend with n-heptane (rated 0) that knocks like the fuel in a standard test engine. The research method (RON) runs at 600 rev/min with a cool intake; the motor method (MON) runs at 900 rev/min with a hot intake and gives a lower number. The difference RON − MON is the fuel's <b>sensitivity</b>."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Air–fuel ratio, λ and blends",
   "blocks": [
    "type",
    "text",
    "where",
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "An SI engine runs near λ = 1 for most of its life so that the three-way catalyst works. Maximum power comes slightly rich (φ about 1.1, the same mixture that burns fastest). Best economy comes slightly lean. Too lean, and the flame becomes slow and unreliable, which is where cycle-to-cycle variation takes over."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Ethanol carries oxygen in its molecule, so it needs less air (stoichiometric air–fuel ratio 9.0) and has less energy per kg (about 26.8 MJ/kg). Its octane number is high, so blends resist knock better. The worked example shows why the energy per kg of <i>mixture</i> barely changes."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Cycle-to-cycle variations",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "At a steady speed, throttle and spark, consecutive traces still do not lie on top of each other. Peak pressure and its crank angle scatter; some cycles burn fast and some slowly. This is <b>cycle-to-cycle variation</b> (cyclic dispersion). The figure shows ten such cycles."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Causes.</b> The early flame kernel is tiny, so whatever happens near the plug gap at the moment of the spark decides how fast the whole cycle burns: the local gas velocity and turbulence, which differ every cycle; the local air–fuel ratio and the amount of residual gas, which are never perfectly uniform; and small differences in spark energy."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Effects.</b> The spark can be set for only the average cycle. Fast cycles then run over-advanced and are the first to knock, so the knock limit is set by the fastest cycles. Slow cycles run retarded and lose work. Variation also limits how lean, how dilute (with EGR) and how high a compression ratio the engine can use,."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Measuring it.</b> The usual measure is the coefficient of variation of the indicated mean effective pressure (IMEP):"
    },
    "type",
    "text",
    "where",
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Reducing it.</b> Make the early flame fast and robust: more turbulence near the plug at the spark (squish, tumble), a central plug and a compact chamber, a higher spark energy or a wider gap, well-mixed charge with less residual gas, and avoiding very lean or very dilute mixtures."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "(a) Find the stoichiometric air–fuel ratio of iso-octane, C<sub>8</sub>H<sub>18</sub>, taking air as 21 % O<sub>2</sub> and 79 % N<sub>2</sub> by volume (molar mass of air 28.97). (b) E20 petrol is 20 % ethanol by volume. Take the densities of ethanol and petrol as 789 and 745 kg/m³, stoichiometric air–fuel ratios 9.0 and 14.7, and calorific values 26.8 and 44 MJ/kg. Find the blend's stoichiometric air–fuel ratio and calorific value, and compare the energy per kg of stoichiometric mixture.",
   "steps": [
    "Balance: C<sub>8</sub>H<sub>18</sub> + a(O<sub>2</sub> + 3.76 N<sub>2</sub>) → 8 CO<sub>2</sub> + 9 H<sub>2</sub>O + 3.76a N<sub>2</sub>. Oxygen: 2a = 16 + 9, so a = 12.5.",
    "Air per kmol of fuel: 12.5 × 4.76 = 59.5 kmol, that is 59.5 × 28.97 = 1723.7 kg.",
    "Fuel: 8 × 12.011 + 18 × 1.008 = 114.23 kg. Air–fuel ratio = 1723.7 ÷ 114.23 = 15.09.",
    "Mass fraction of ethanol in E20: (0.2 × 789) ÷ (0.2 × 789 + 0.8 × 745) = 157.8 ÷ 753.8 = 0.209.",
    "Blend air–fuel ratio (by mass): 0.209 × 9.0 + 0.791 × 14.7 = 1.88 + 11.63 = 13.5.",
    "Blend calorific value: 0.209 × 26.8 + 0.791 × 44 = 5.61 + 34.79 = 40.4 MJ/kg, about 8 % less than petrol.",
    "Energy per kg of stoichiometric mixture: petrol 44 ÷ (14.7 + 1) = 2.80 MJ/kg; E20 40.4 ÷ (13.5 + 1) = 2.79 MJ/kg."
   ],
   "answer": "(a) 15.1 : 1. (b) E20 needs 13.5 : 1 and gives 40.4 MJ/kg, yet a cylinder full of stoichiometric E20 mixture carries almost the same energy as petrol (2.79 against 2.80 MJ/kg); the engine simply injects about 8 % more fuel by mass."
  },
  {
   "question": "Ten consecutive cycles of the engine in the figure give IMEP (bar): 9.40, 9.51, 9.23, 9.44, 9.31, 9.44, 9.14, 9.52, 9.38, 9.34. (a) Find the mean, the standard deviation and COV<sub>imep</sub>. (b) The peak pressures of the same cycles range from 42.0 to 56.3 bar with a COV of 9.1 %. Comment.",
   "steps": [
    "Mean = 93.71 ÷ 10 = 9.371 bar.",
    "Deviations from the mean: 0.029, 0.139, −0.141, 0.069, −0.061, 0.069, −0.231, 0.149, 0.009, −0.031.",
    "Sum of squares = 0.1299 bar².",
    "Standard deviation (n − 1 = 9): s = √(0.1299 ÷ 9) = √0.01443 = 0.120 bar.",
    "COV<sub>imep</sub> = 0.120 ÷ 9.371 × 100 = 1.28 %.",
    "Peak pressure scatters seven times more (9.1 %) because it depends strongly on where the burn falls; IMEP averages the whole cycle, and an early cycle's extra peak is partly paid back as less work later."
   ],
   "answer": "(a) Mean 9.37 bar, s = 0.120 bar, COV<sub>imep</sub> = 1.3 %, well below the 10 % limit, so the engine runs smoothly. (b) Peak pressure is a much more sensitive, but less useful, measure of variation."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Octane number is a volume percentage of iso-octane in a reference blend, not a percentage of iso-octane in the petrol itself.",
  "MON is lower than RON for the same fuel because the motor test is harsher; sensitivity is RON − MON, not MON − RON.",
  "The 10 % point governs cold starting and vapour lock, the 90 % point governs oil dilution and deposits. Swapping them is a common slip.",
  "A lower calorific value per kg does not mean less energy per cylinder: what counts is the energy per kg of mixture.",
  "Use the standard deviation, not the range, in COV<sub>imep</sub>, and divide by the mean IMEP, not by the peak pressure."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Name four properties of gasoline that affect engine operation.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Any four: volatility (distillation curve, Reid vapour pressure), octane number, calorific value, density, stoichiometric air–fuel ratio, latent heat, sulphur and gum content."
  },
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define COV<sub>imep</sub>.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "The standard deviation of IMEP over many cycles divided by the mean IMEP, as a percentage."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does the knock limit of an engine depend on cycle-to-cycle variation?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The spark is set for the average cycle, so the fastest-burning cycles are over-advanced and knock first; the larger the scatter, the more the average timing must be retarded."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "What does too high a front-end volatility do in hot weather?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Fuel boils in the lines or pump, vapour lock starves the engine of liquid fuel, and the engine stalls or will not restart hot."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "An engine runs at an air–fuel ratio of 13.2 on petrol (stoichiometric 14.7). Find λ and φ.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "λ = 13.2 ÷ 14.7 = 0.898; φ = 1/λ = 1.114, a rich mixture."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Five lean cycles give IMEP 7.57, 6.76, 7.41, 5.37 and 6.14 bar. Find COV<sub>imep</sub>.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Mean 6.65 bar; sum of squared deviations 3.335; s = √(3.335 ÷ 4) = 0.913 bar; COV = 13.7 %, above the 10 % limit."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "An engine that ran smoothly at λ = 1 becomes rough at λ = 1.4. Explain with the flame kernel.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "A lean charge has a slower laminar burning velocity and a longer lag, so the kernel is more at the mercy of local turbulence and mixture near the plug; some cycles burn slowly or partly, IMEP scatters and COV rises past 10 %."
  }
 ],
